418
00:46:23,870 --> 00:46:27,150
Brand safety defined once in knowledge catalog.

419
00:46:28,174 --> 00:46:36,610
That becomes a shared meaning that your agents share to talk in the common language, which is the semantic alignment part of it.

420
00:46:37,230 --> 00:46:45,070
The planning graph in Spanner and the path behind every set aside becomes a trace record that you can revert back to.

421
00:46:45,742 --> 00:46:51,010
And the record, who proposed, who signed, under which rules, that all becomes a graph.

422
00:46:52,078 --> 00:46:58,970
And BigQuery graph tracing a purchase back to that same decision, every decision kept as evidence.

423
00:47:00,046 --> 00:47:01,970
And the agent keeps learning.

424
00:47:02,670 --> 00:47:09,030
Every run leaves a trace in the record, the options it weighed, the one it chose and why.

425
00:47:10,158 --> 00:47:13,790
The agent development kit streams every step to BigQuery.

426
00:47:14,958 --> 00:47:22,510
We train our models on those traces, including graph neural networks with Google's open source distributed graph flow.

427
00:47:23,246 --> 00:47:27,490
So the next run starts from more evidence and more understanding.

428
00:47:28,782 --> 00:47:30,830
That's why knowledge comes first.

429
00:47:30,830 --> 00:47:37,930
In our test, a general-purpose model with no access to our catalog named hundreds of ad units.

430
00:47:38,330 --> 00:47:39,090
None was ours.

431
00:47:40,050 --> 00:47:43,070
Grounded in our knowledge catalog, it invented none.

432
00:47:45,838 --> 00:47:49,580
Following every rule in a separate test and we keep measuring it.

433
00:47:50,030 --> 00:47:54,180
That significantly reduced hallucination and provides strong grounding.

434
00:47:54,860 --> 00:47:57,620
And here's what I am proudest of.

435
00:47:58,180 --> 00:48:00,600
Technical agents build our business agents.

436
00:48:01,380 --> 00:48:02,540
We set a goal.

437
00:48:03,438 --> 00:48:09,360
Every layer of our medallion architecture spins up its own pipeline just in time.

438
00:48:10,300 --> 00:48:18,420
Bronze lends our raw data, silver makes it information, gold claims we can check and platinum the knowledge.

439
00:48:19,160 --> 00:48:23,740
From inference to understanding to action to trust, a workflow of workflows.

440
00:48:24,340 --> 00:48:28,180
Our coding agents call Google's data agent kit as a skill.

441
00:48:29,160 --> 00:48:31,720
Models aren't trained on Google's new as tools.

442
00:48:32,302 --> 00:48:35,660
So the kit gets the syntax right the first time.

443
00:48:35,800 --> 00:48:39,280
It helps bring the grounding to our coding agent.

444
00:48:40,000 --> 00:48:45,800
BigQuery's data engineering agent and the query data API do the rest guided by our knowledge graph.

445
00:48:46,446 --> 00:48:49,420
Our engineers set the intent and check the work.

446
00:48:50,080 --> 00:48:51,860
So what did this blueprint give us?

447
00:48:53,040 --> 00:48:53,400
Grounding.

448
00:48:53,960 --> 00:48:55,320
New Mexico means the state.

449
00:48:55,854 --> 00:49:00,340
Every time and in our test, no invented ad units.

450
00:49:01,540 --> 00:49:04,620
Adherence, the agent stops where a person must decide.

451
00:49:06,020 --> 00:49:12,940
Graph reg, search that also follows the graph's links, the right units and audiences with the reasons attached.

452
00:49:16,230 --> 00:49:18,070
Context assembled just in time.

453
00:49:19,090 --> 00:49:24,430
Plain English to SQL for anyone and the right model for each step.

454
00:49:25,198 --> 00:49:28,810
And action with a person's approval measured on incremental lift.

455
00:49:28,814 --> 00:49:32,110
Now, none of these works in pieces.

456
00:49:33,130 --> 00:49:36,410
So why Google's agentic data cloud, one may ask.

457
00:49:36,974 --> 00:49:43,350
Because one foundation connects all five layers from decision to action to evidence.

458
00:49:44,046 --> 00:49:49,190
It scales to petabytes with low latency and reliability you need when money moves.

459
00:49:49,830 --> 00:49:54,670
Data and intelligence powered by an AI native data platform with seamless access.

460
00:49:55,438 --> 00:49:56,390
And that's powerful.

461
00:49:56,390 --> 00:50:01,070
So, it needs strong governance and it must be cost-effective as well.

462
00:50:01,750 --> 00:50:02,750
And it moves fast.

463
00:50:02,870 --> 00:50:09,690
When we pushed for BigQuery graph and conversational analytics in Looker, Google worked with us to ship them at lightning speed.

464
00:50:10,062 --> 00:50:11,590
And most of all, it compounds.

465
00:50:12,270 --> 00:50:17,010
Every run adds evidence and we test each improvement before we adopt it.

466
00:50:17,742 --> 00:50:19,790
For Yahoo, that unlocked new business.

467
00:50:20,350 --> 00:50:23,130
For a small business, the moment popped down the street.

468
00:50:23,130 --> 00:50:28,410
We started with a small team of Direct campaigns started at $50,000 and now they start at a few thousand.

469
00:50:29,134 --> 00:50:31,310
That enabled us to do a TAM expansion.

470
00:50:31,990 --> 00:50:33,390
None of this was built alone.

471
00:50:34,050 --> 00:50:39,428
Yahoo's engineers and Google's teams worked as one team side by side week after week.

472
00:50:40,302 --> 00:50:44,310
Google brought its newest technology, we brought a hard real problem.

473
00:50:45,150 --> 00:50:48,450
We asked they built and we together fast.

474
00:50:49,102 --> 00:50:52,430
To both teams, I am proud of what we have built together.

475
00:50:53,454 --> 00:50:56,950
Whatever you are building, start in the same place we did.

476
00:50:57,410 --> 00:50:57,910
The data.

477
00:50:58,670 --> 00:51:01,210
The blueprint is on the Google Cloud blog.

478
00:51:01,838 --> 00:51:03,490
So let's go back to Friday night.

479
00:51:03,950 --> 00:51:05,190
Your phone buzzes.

480
00:51:05,806 --> 00:51:06,510
$50,000?

481
00:51:07,438 --> 00:51:08,190
Would you let it?

482
00:51:08,750 --> 00:51:09,270
I would.

483
00:51:10,290 --> 00:51:13,930
When it's grounded in meaning, so it understands what I meant.

484
00:51:14,318 --> 00:51:18,510
When it adheres to the steps and stops when a person must decide.

485
00:51:19,130 --> 00:51:23,430
And when it's governed by rules and a person signs before money moves.

486
00:51:24,050 --> 00:51:26,430
When the outcome is measured against a holdout.

487
00:51:27,150 --> 00:51:31,350
And when it's provenance, one record connects it all.

488
00:51:32,070 --> 00:51:34,930
Meaning, procedure, authority, and the record.

489
00:51:35,290 --> 00:51:37,190
Four ontologies capped as data.

490
00:51:37,646 --> 00:51:40,910
That's an agent that's safe and in control.

491
00:51:40,910 --> 00:51:45,870
Google, taking real action towards real outcome on Google's agentic data cloud.

492
00:51:46,590 --> 00:51:49,890
The blueprint, spanner to act and BigQuery to learn.

493
00:51:50,870 --> 00:51:55,370
Built together with Google on one idea that trust starts with the data.

494
00:51:56,462 --> 00:51:58,850
Because trust isn't something an agent has.

495
00:51:59,598 --> 00:52:02,750
It's something we build one decision at a time.

496
00:52:03,470 --> 00:52:05,190
Trusted agents and real outcomes.

497
00:52:05,902 --> 00:52:06,350
Thank you.

498
00:52:06,350 --> 00:52:12,230
Up next, Ryan and Andy dive into some of your top questions in the expert Q&A.

499
00:52:23,470 --> 00:52:28,790
Thanks, Mikul, and it's really awesome to see what you and Yahoo have been able to accomplish with agents and advertising.

500
00:52:29,390 --> 00:52:30,850
And welcome back, everyone.

501
00:52:31,534 --> 00:52:35,950
For this segment, we're going to answer the top questions that you submitted during registration.

502
00:52:36,350 --> 00:52:40,230
And who better to answer than the architect of the Agentic Data Cloud?

503
00:52:40,350 --> 00:52:44,510
So please welcome back Andy Gutmanns, VP and General Manager of Data Cloud.

504
00:52:46,620 --> 00:52:48,840
Andy, I'm looking forward to digging into these questions.

505
00:52:49,140 --> 00:52:50,280
Thank you so much for having me.

506
00:52:50,440 --> 00:52:52,040
The first question that we have, and we got a lot.

507
00:52:52,160 --> 00:52:59,980
This one comes from a data engineer in the UK, and their question is, how do you envision the future for people who work with data?

508
00:53:00,320 --> 00:53:05,580
Especially when agents can independently explore data sets and find insights and generate reports.

509
00:53:06,160 --> 00:53:09,180
I think this is a super exciting time for folks who work with data.

510
00:53:09,960 --> 00:53:23,700
If you think about the strengths we bring to the table, it's the business understanding, the domain expertise, the creativity, the judgment, but we spend so much of our time on like the gory details and the toil.

511
00:53:24,340 --> 00:53:30,140
I think what's happening now with agents is we can actually really focus on the business outcomes we're trying to drive.

512
00:53:30,620 --> 00:53:41,420
And I think I call that intent-driven outcomes where we're basically building agents for practitioners where practitioners can actually use their judgment and their understanding to give a much higher level goal.

513
00:53:41,710 --> 00:53:45,880
And then actually have the agents go and do a lot of that toil and busy work for them.

514
00:53:46,862 --> 00:53:56,200
And so you're really seeing that practitioner moving from just being an individual contributor to being an orchestrator of agents and using that business understanding.

515
00:53:56,400 --> 00:53:57,720
So I think it's super exciting.

516
00:53:58,260 --> 00:53:59,040
It is an exciting time.

517
00:53:59,220 --> 00:53:59,960
Yeah, thank you for that.

518
00:54:00,800 --> 00:54:06,060
This one comes from a senior vice president of AI and automation based in the United States.

519
00:54:06,606 --> 00:54:15,040
And their question is, is it an absolute that my enterprise data must live in Google to have a Gemini agentic workforce?

520
00:54:15,800 --> 00:54:17,080
Yeah, that's an awesome question.

521
00:54:17,902 --> 00:54:21,520
I always say that the enterprise data state is super messy.

522
00:54:23,180 --> 00:54:32,980
Customers have data on-premises, a lot of our customers have multi-Cloud postures, and so there's data sprawl, data is structured, it's unstructured.

523
00:54:33,840 --> 00:54:40,020
So as you think about agents and the agentic AI, you have to be able to activate all of your data for agents.

524
00:54:40,020 --> 00:54:53,160
So if we told our customers, which by the way, a lot of vendors do, they tell their customers, you've got to bring all the data into my lake house or my environment, and then I'm going to activate it with agents, that just doesn't make business sense.

525
00:54:53,760 --> 00:55:09,240
So we basically launched this thing called Borderless Lakehouse that tears down those walled gardens and we're able to activate data no matter where it sits, whether it sits on GCP, it could be sitting on Azure, on AWS, even on-premises and operational databases and SaaS applications.

526
00:55:10,260 --> 00:55:21,380
And so Google is really taking a very differentiated approach here to help customers, you know, get to value with AI without having to kind of move all of their data into a single place.

527
00:55:21,800 --> 00:55:21,920
Right.

528
00:55:22,382 --> 00:55:23,100
Thank you for that.

529
00:55:23,440 --> 00:55:24,520
Okay, let's go to the next question.

530
00:55:24,680 --> 00:55:27,720
This one, in some cases, we got a lot of questions.

531
00:55:27,780 --> 00:55:31,200
And so what we did is we aggregated them to get the spirit of the question.

532
00:55:31,300 --> 00:55:38,080
And so this one is, how do you assess and limit agent permissions based on risk.

533
00:55:38,440 --> 00:55:46,300
One engineer put it this way, they said, talk to me about the relationship between my agents in the cloud and system security protocols.

534
00:55:46,300 --> 00:55:48,480
It seems tricky to square those right now.

535
00:55:48,720 --> 00:55:50,080
Yeah, no, first of all, that's a great question.

536
00:55:50,140 --> 00:55:53,180
I think before I talk about the agent itself, let me just talk about the platform.

537
00:55:53,400 --> 00:55:59,700
I think one of the big benefits you get with Google Cloud is we're the only ones who own the full stack.

538
00:56:00,060 --> 00:56:05,340
You know, we own the model, we own the data the platform, the infrastructure, the AI inferencing with our TPUs.

539
00:56:06,080 --> 00:56:10,360
And the way we've built this stack is a single security model, right?

540
00:56:10,440 --> 00:56:15,360
So, the security flows through from the agent itself all the way down to the data processing layer.

541
00:56:15,950 --> 00:56:20,860
If you think about other vendors, they usually have to either rent the infrastructure, rent the model or rent both.

542
00:56:21,340 --> 00:56:26,020
And so, they tend to have to build a bolt-on security capability.

543
00:56:26,600 --> 00:56:30,260
And that bolt-on can leave certain risks.

544
00:56:30,260 --> 00:56:33,220
And so that's really a big part of our differentiation.

545
00:56:34,400 --> 00:56:44,460
And so, and what we've done in the agent platform is, you know, we're enabling you to launch agents with the least amount of permissions that the agent needs to get the job done.

546
00:56:44,660 --> 00:56:46,600
And again, that flows all the way through the platform.

547
00:56:46,800 --> 00:57:00,600
So we're really making sure that, you know, only giving agents, even if the agent is working on your behalf, not giving them all the permissions that you have, but really, you know, narrowing it down to only what's needed to get the job done.

548
00:57:01,420 --> 00:57:02,960
So that's just super critical.

549
00:57:03,360 --> 00:57:10,560
Then we also have a whole governance layer that actually allows our customers to control those environments and control the permissions and access controls.

550
00:57:11,160 --> 00:57:11,740
Well, thank you.

551
00:57:12,622 --> 00:57:17,220
This is another question that was asked by many people and we've aggregated this again.

552
00:57:17,678 --> 00:57:22,480
The question is essentially, what information should live in that semantic layer?

553
00:57:23,040 --> 00:57:33,160
So for example, is having a good implementation of knowledge catalog metadata essential for the effective use of that tool when you're trying to establish that semantic layer?

554
00:57:33,620 --> 00:57:34,280
Yeah, no, absolutely.

555
00:57:34,500 --> 00:57:38,740
And I think, you know, there's kind of three things that really matter to customers.

556
00:57:38,960 --> 00:57:42,440
Number one is you want to get to the best quality outcome, right?

557
00:57:42,460 --> 00:57:44,160
So quality and trust really matters.

558
00:57:44,560 --> 00:57:46,900
But you also want to be able to do that at the lowest cost.

559
00:57:47,758 --> 00:57:51,360
And also with the right user experience, which also means the lowest latency, right?

560
00:57:51,360 --> 00:57:57,900
because even if you get the right answer, if it takes two hours to get the response, it's gonna be a terrible user experience.

561
00:57:58,400 --> 00:58:00,960
And so the question there is, how can you optimize that?

562
00:58:01,454 --> 00:58:11,400
One of the ways to optimize that is to give the model the highest quality context and only the context that matters to driving that outcome.

563
00:58:11,780 --> 00:58:25,960
And this is where Knowledge Catalog is so critical because not only do we aggregate and enrich the context and really understand the enterprise setting and context, but also the retrieval only retrieves the piece of the context that matters the most.

564
00:58:26,100 --> 00:58:39,100
And so what ends up happening is you have less input tokens, you know, less reasoning loops, which also means less tool calls, which means lower costs, lower latency, and of course, high quality, because we're giving the highest quality outcome.

565
00:58:39,982 --> 00:58:50,120
So having, you know, really using something like a knowledge catalog to really make sure where the knowledge of how the enterprise operates, how the data relates to itself is super critical.

566
00:58:51,020 --> 00:58:56,980
And that's a super important part of the agentic data cloud and what we're working with customers on.

567
00:58:57,280 --> 00:58:57,620
Yeah, that's cool.

568
00:58:57,660 --> 00:59:00,020
It's not just context, but it's efficient context.

569
00:59:00,420 --> 00:59:01,120
Exactly, exactly.

570
00:59:01,260 --> 00:59:03,960
And you know, it's not a zero-sum game.

571
00:59:04,080 --> 00:59:07,440
You can get the higher quality and the lower cost together.

572
00:59:07,620 --> 00:59:08,520
You don't have to compromise.

573
00:59:08,860 --> 00:59:09,480
Right, right.

574
00:59:10,222 --> 00:59:18,120
This one also came from a lot of registrants, which is how do you keep costs under control?

575
00:59:18,300 --> 00:59:20,520
This is something I think on a lot of people's minds these days.

576
00:59:20,560 --> 00:59:26,460
How do you keep costs under control when you're moving from a few co-pilots to thousands of agents or more?

577
00:59:26,620 --> 00:59:27,660
Yeah, that's a great question.

578
00:59:27,840 --> 00:59:36,760
And you know, I sometimes refer to it as the million agent question because we're gonna have a million agents in production and that could be very expensive, right?

579
00:59:36,820 --> 00:59:38,060
Unless you have the right platform.

580
00:59:38,180 --> 00:59:41,040
And there are a lot of different elements that actually go into that.

581
00:59:41,040 --> 00:59:46,040
If I just start with what our Gemini team is building, right, we're building different models.

582
00:59:47,100 --> 00:59:56,820
And some models are, of course, you have the Gemini Flash, we have Gemini Pro, and I always say the best model is the model that gets the outcome you need at the lowest cost, right?

583
00:59:57,038 --> 01:00:08,040
And so part of what we're also building in Gemini is making sure that we actually automatically select the model, right, that is the right model to use so we can keep your cost down, but we don't compromise on the outcome.

584
01:00:08,180 --> 01:00:11,396
So one is the model selection and how the harness works.

585
01:00:12,000 --> 01:00:14,540
And that's a big area of investment for us.

586
01:00:14,990 --> 01:00:17,560
The other piece that I already mentioned is the context.

587
01:00:18,340 --> 01:00:25,000
Having the right context really helps you not only get to that high quality outcome, but do it at a much lower cost.

588
01:00:25,120 --> 01:00:33,880
We tend to see customers who are using Knowledge Catalog see over 50% lower token costs than if they're not using Knowledge Catalog.

589
01:00:33,940 --> 01:00:35,540
So it's a huge cost saver for them.

590
01:00:36,238 --> 01:00:37,540
So I think that's the second piece.

591
01:00:37,550 --> 01:00:43,960
And the third one is, is your data platform actually architected, right, for this million agent problem?

592
01:00:44,440 --> 01:00:53,440
And when you have a huge amount of, you know, if you have a huge surge in agents trying to get to an operational database, as an example, you know, do you have to over provision and break the bank?

593
01:00:53,934 --> 01:00:56,240
Or can we handle that in a very cost effective way?

594
01:00:56,300 --> 01:01:05,000
So for example, you know, when we announced Postgres for agents in AlloyDB, a big part of the goal there was to actually support agent scale.

595
01:01:05,560 --> 01:01:20,400
support these agentic experiences where we may have huge peaks of agents accessing the Postgres database, but not needing to over-provision and just provision to the degree that these agents need.

596
01:01:20,500 --> 01:01:25,320
And when the agents kind of finish their job, we can scale down that environment super quickly.

597
01:01:25,520 --> 01:01:27,500
So lots of different components to that.

598
01:01:28,180 --> 01:01:36,940
As I mentioned before, I think one of the big advantages we see customers getting from Google Cloud is the fact that we have that whole stack.

599
01:01:37,358 --> 01:01:41,280
We're able to integrate the whole stack and that allows us to drive cost savings.

600
01:01:42,020 --> 01:01:42,600
Thank you.

601
01:01:43,150 --> 01:01:49,600
This is the last question we'll do today, which is, again, this was really asked by a lot of people.

602
01:01:49,940 --> 01:01:55,200
The gist of it is how do you achieve ultra high performance for agents?

603
01:01:56,000 --> 01:01:56,120
Yeah.

604
01:01:56,220 --> 01:02:00,560
So, I mean, you know, ultra high performance, you know, is always a hard thing.

605
01:02:00,560 --> 01:02:08,020
A lot of that goes back to what I mentioned before, which is choose the right model for the job and we help you choose that, which is very important.

606
01:02:08,600 --> 01:02:24,460
We're also, you know, as we're building plugins for like operational reporting and analytics, if you think about it, if you're in finance and you want to see what your revenue is in a certain region this week, it's highly likely you're going to want to ask the same question next week, right?

607
01:02:24,540 --> 01:02:35,440
And so the other piece we're doing is, you know, if we have these kind of very certified like queries that we know you're going to want again, we can actually skip the whole auto generation of that query next time, right?

608
01:02:35,520 --> 01:02:38,380
And completely save all the token costs, right?

609
01:02:38,440 --> 01:02:42,340
So I think there's a lot of, a lot of inherent work we're putting into that.

610
01:02:42,574 --> 01:02:48,020
And of course that also gets you to ultra high performance because now you don't have to invoke an LLM, right?

611
01:02:48,270 --> 01:02:49,260
To generate the query.

612
01:02:49,860 --> 01:02:52,140
So I think there's a lot of different pieces of the stack.

613
01:02:52,280 --> 01:02:54,840
One is like using the best model for the job.

614
01:02:55,310 --> 01:03:02,020
Second is even skipping the model when you can, as I mentioned, with this kind of query caching or so on.

615
01:03:02,740 --> 01:03:07,360
And then I think a lot of this also matters, depends on the data platform itself.

616
01:03:08,420 --> 01:03:13,180
You know, we architected Google Cloud very differently than everyone else, right?

617
01:03:13,200 --> 01:03:15,320
We had to support the greater Google business.

618
01:03:15,694 --> 01:03:18,700
We have disaggregated compute and storage architecture.

619
01:03:18,940 --> 01:03:22,740
Latencies really matter to us, you know, in the search business, the ads business, YouTube.

620
01:03:22,740 --> 01:03:25,560
And so we already built for ultra low latency.

621
01:03:26,062 --> 01:03:30,780
So I think also the data access path, we tend to do extremely well on latency.

622
01:03:31,040 --> 01:03:38,300
And so that helps us kind of really reduce the time it takes for agents to actually get the work done.

623
01:03:38,880 --> 01:03:40,620
You know, we're never done.

624
01:03:41,380 --> 01:03:46,040
You can really bet on us continuing to work both on performance and on price performance.

625
01:03:46,574 --> 01:03:47,160
Thanks, Andy.

626
01:03:47,694 --> 01:03:49,380
And thank you all for those great questions.

627
01:03:49,520 --> 01:03:51,880
We so appreciate hearing all of your voices.

